    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1221">

                <pb facs="1325" n="1315" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1315"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.6266A/10: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Representative to the French Committee of National Liberation at
                        Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>)<anchor n="59" xml:id="d1221-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-02T21:00:00-04:00">March 2, 1944—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>659. Your 559, February 21, noon. For Reinhardt.<anchor n="60" xml:id="d1221-f2"
                    /> The Department believes that the treatment to be accorded by Allied
                    authorities to diplomats of enemy powers accredited to the Holy See should not
                    be less favorable than that accorded to us during the Fascist regime and
                    subsequently during the German occupation of Rome. The former Fascist regime
                    permitted diplomats of nations at war with Italy and accredited to Holy See to
                    move into Vatican City and remain there unmolested. The Department considers
                    that the Germans, Japanese and other enemy diplomatic missions accredited to
                    Holy See should be given the option of entering Vatican City upon the arrival of
                    Allied troops in the Italian capital.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1221-f1" n="59" type="source" xml:id="d1221-f1-content">
                <p>Transmitted as telegram 917, March 20, 1944, to Bern for the Chargé at Vatican
                    City, Harold H. Tittmann.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1221-f2" n="60" xml:id="d1221-f2-content">
                <p>G. Frederick Bernhardt, Assistant to Robert D. Murphy, the United States
                    Political Adviser at Allied Force Headquarters.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
